  "Question": "The number of real values of \\(x\\) satisfying the equality \\((\\log_a x)(\\log_b x)=\\log_a b,\\) where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive constants different from \\(1,\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "an integer greater than \\(2\\)",
    "E": "not finite"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The logarithms require \\(x>0\\). To combine the two different bases, let \\(t=\\log_b x\\). By change of base,\n\\[\n\\log_a x=(\\log_a b)t.\n\\]\nAlso, \\(\\log_a b\\neq0\\), because a logarithm equal to zero would give \\(b=a^0=1\\), which is excluded. Substitution into the given equation therefore gives\n\\[\n(\\log_a b)t^2=\\log_a b,\n\\qquad t^2=1.\n\\]\nBoth square-root possibilities must be included: \\(t=1\\) or \\(t=-1\\). Converting back from logarithms yields\n\\[\nx=b\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=b^{-1}=\\frac1b.\n\\]\nBoth values are positive. They are distinct, since \\(b=1/b\\) with \\(b>0\\) would force \\(b=1\\). Every transformation was reversible on \\(x>0\\), so there are exactly \\(\\boxed{2}\\) real solutions, choice C.",
